! Buchholz Appendix B, Table B.2 "Cube-root Extraction", printed p.297
! (PDF 322), WITH ONE STATEMENT SUBSTITUTED.  The table, its notes and
! every mapping are in decks/buchholz_b2_cube_root.strap, which is the
! book's program as printed; read that header first.
!
! THE SUBSTITUTION, AND ITS LICENCE.  The model does not build VFL
! STORE ROUNDED (decks/repro_vfl_srd.strap, docs/NEXT.md).  The printed
!
!   105.32  STORE ROUNDED (VB, 12, 1), 8.0, 117      SRD(B,12,1), 8.0, 117
!
! is replaced here by
!
!           +MGI(BU,1,8), 1, 116
!           ST(B,12,1), 8.0, 117
!
! on the word of Reference Manual 22-6530-2 printed p.64 (PDF 67),
! Store Rounded, PROGRAMMING NOTES: "The accumulator contents can be
! rounded by using ADD TO MAGNITUDE with an immediate address of 1 or 5
! with the proper offset", the proper offset being the one that puts
! the 1 "in the bit position immediately to the right of the specified
! offset" (same page, Radix): offset 116 for a store at 117.  The pair
! differs from SRD in one way the program cannot see: it rounds the
! accumulator itself, where SRD leaves the accumulator alone -- but the
! store is INTO the accumulator (8.0), so both leave the same bits
! there.
!
! THIS IS NOT THE BOOK'S PROGRAM.  It is one word longer, so everything
! after 105.32 sits one word on: the loop top is 108.32 where the table
! has 107.32, and the stop is 113.0 where the table has 112.0.  What it
! tests is everything in Table B.2 except SRD: E+I, F+, R/, the signed
! VFL load with a one-bit sign byte, VFL /I, a VFL store into the
! accumulator's exponent, and the book's claim of "full 48-bit accuracy
! for any N in three iterations ... except for a possible rounding
! error in the last iteration".
!
! THE CHECK IS tests/verify_buchholz_b2.py substituted, which runs this
! deck for eight values of N (it rewrites the "DATA, N" line) and
! applies the book's tolerance; its header has the values and the
! arithmetic.  The N written below, 100, is the first of them:
! x_root must be (16)0069487E54E3B0F0 give or take one in the last
! fraction place (worked in the printed deck's header).
!
! INJECTIONS, DATA SIDE, OBSERVED, through the verifier (which takes a
! second argument naming a changed copy of this deck; clean: 8 of 8
! values of N, 32 of 32 checks):
!
!   f0 changed from 0.7109375 to 0.5 ((16)0008000000000000 at 202.0):
!   FAILS 2 of 32, x_root for N = 0.75*2**-6 and for the largest
!   fraction.  The other six still converge.  That is the page's own
!   account of f0: "selected to give about equal iteration errors at
!   the extreme values of the final fraction".
!
!   The index word's count changed from 3 to 2 (two iterations):
!   FAILS 6 of 32, x_root for every N but 3 and 0.375.

        SLC, 100.0
START   LX, $1, 200.0                 ! Start
        L(U), 204.0
        E+I(U), -1                    ! (1)
        +(N), 204.0
        ST(U), 203.0                  ! (2)
        L(B,12,1), 204.0, 117         ! (3)
        +I(BU,1,8), 1, 117            ! (4)
        /I(BU,2,8), (2)11, 116        ! (5)
        +MGI(BU,1,8), 1, 116          ! (6) SUBSTITUTED: round ...
        ST(B,12,1), 8.0, 117          ! (6) SUBSTITUTED: ... and store
        F+(U), 202.0                  ! (7)
        ST(U), 205.0                  ! (8)
        *(N), 205.0
        *(N), 205.0
        E+I(U), 1                     ! (9)
        +(N), 204.0
        R/(N), 203.0                  ! (10)
        +(N), 201.0                   ! (11)
        *(N), 205.0
        ST(U), 205.0                  ! (12)
        CB, $1, 108.32                ! (13) the loop top, one word on
        BEW, 113.0                    ! Stop, one word on

        SLC, 200.0
        XW, 0.0, 3, 200.0                      ! INDEX, 0.0, 3, 200.0
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)0008000000000000      ! DATA, 0.5 E 0
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)000B600000000000      ! DATA, 0.7109375 E 0
        DD(BU,64,8), 0                         ! DATA
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)00EC800000000000      ! DATA, N
        DD(BU,64,8), 0                         ! DATA, X

        END, START
